#!/root/agent/venv/bin/python
"""category-rank -- where our Actors land in apify.com/store CATEGORY BROWSE.

SCOPE: this measures the BROWSE surface (store sidebar category -> list), which is a
different surface from `bin/store-rank` (the search BOX). Both are the public Algolia
index `prod_PUBLIC_STORE`; neither is `bin/store-visibility` (/v2/store REST, NOT
buyer-facing -- see that tool's scope banner).

Mechanics (cycle 581 measured, cycle 582 validated end-to-end): category browse with an
empty query is ordered by `storePosition` ascending, so an Actor's browse rank inside a
category is exactly:

    rank = count(listings in that category with storePosition < ours) + 1

which is one Algolia call with `numericFilters` -- O(1), no paging. Validated twice
against the real ordered listing: `scholarship-scraper` predicted p206/390 in EDUCATION
and measured p206; `steam-reviews-scraper` predicted p81/110 in GAMES and, after the
category was actually shipped, measured p81. Trust the prediction.

`categories` lives in `actors/<slug>/meta.json` (not in `.actor/actor.json`), ships with
`apify-admin publish`, and is a free field we fully control -- unlike `storePosition`,
which is Apify-computed from cumulative usage. So filing an Actor in a SMALL category is
the one browse-side lever we have. Caveat on expected value: a good browse rank in a
109-listing category makes us reachable by someone who browses that category, not
featured. Ship, then watch `users30d` before recategorizing the whole fleet.

NOTE: the index takes ~1-2 minutes to reflect a category change after
`apify-admin publish` + `apify push --force`. A query run immediately after can still
show the old category set and the old facet count; re-run before concluding anything.

CEILING / WHY THIS LEVER IS CLOSED (cycle 1620 -- read before "optimising" categories):
browse rank is not a property of the category you pick, it tracks our Apify-computed
`storePosition`, which is deep in the store's tail. Measured fleet-wide at 1620 -- every
Actor's best rank among its own current categories, and what is reachable at all:

    the ONLY page-1-feasible categories store-wide are COVID_19 (7 listings) and
    DEVELOPER_EXAMPLES (7). Every other category runs 155 -> 32141 listings, and we
    land near the bottom of each: GAMES p120-125/155, FOR_CREATORS p247-293/310,
    EDUCATION p492-628/648, JOBS p6155/7458, BUSINESS p5409-8833/9369,
    NEWS p3886/4746, LEAD_GENERATION p30688-31250/31440, DEVELOPER_TOOLS p24351+/24762.

5 of 24 Actors hold a page-1 browse slot, all of them via COVID_19 (us-federal-awards p1,
fda-recall p2, nih-reporter p3, clinicaltrials p4, federal-register p5 of 7 -- and 5 of
those 7 listings are ours). For the other 19 there is no honest way to reach page 1:
COVID_19 would be topical spam on a game/podcast/jobs scraper, and DEVELOPER_EXAMPLES is
dishonest fleet-wide since we sell products, not demos (CLAUDE.md rule 1).

=> `category_lever` is CLOSED: structurally exhausted, not "not yet due". Adding or
swapping a category cannot improve browse reach until `storePosition` improves, and
storePosition is Apify-computed from cumulative usage -- so it follows users, it cannot
lead them. Run `--ceiling` to re-derive this from live numbers before disbelieving it.
Categories still matter for the SEARCH index (indexed verbatim, cycle 1468), so filling
an empty slot with a topically honest category is a free marginal search-matching gain
-- just do not score it as a browse-rank win.

DO NOT compute this from a store-wide storePosition percentile: those counts come back
`exhaustiveNbHits=False` and are wrong, not merely noisy. Measured 1620:
`storePosition<34312` reports 80472 of 86132 listings, impossible when only 69255
listings sit outside ECOMMERCE and just 7092 ECOMMERCE members rank ahead of that same
Actor. Small categories DO return exhaustive counts, so per-category ranks are sound;
only the store-wide denominator is unusable. `--ceiling` probes real categories instead.

Usage:
  bin/category-rank                 # every registry Actor: rank in each of its categories
  bin/category-rank <slug>          # one Actor
  bin/category-rank --all <slug>    # also score every OTHER category (what-if table)
  bin/category-rank --facets        # just the store-wide category sizes, smallest first
  bin/category-rank --ceiling       # page-1 feasibility per Actor (re-derives the above)
"""
import sys, json, urllib.request

APP, KEY, IDX = "OW0O5I3QO7", "0ecccd09f50396a4dbbe5dbfb17f4525", "prod_PUBLIC_STORE"
REG = "/root/agent/actors/registry.json"

# --ceiling: how many of the smallest categories to probe per Actor (1 Algolia call each).
K_PROBE = 8
# Categories we will not file under even if they would rank well: our Actors are
# commercial products, not demos, so DEVELOPER_EXAMPLES would be a deceptive listing
# (CLAUDE.md rule 1). Flagged rather than silently dropped so the arithmetic stays visible.
DISHONEST = {"DEVELOPER_EXAMPLES"}


def algolia(body):
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        f"https://{APP}-dsn.algolia.net/1/indexes/{IDX}/query",
        data=json.dumps(body).encode(),
        headers={"X-Algolia-Application-Id": APP, "X-Algolia-API-Key": KEY,
                 "Content-Type": "application/json"})
    return json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req))


def facet_sizes():
    r = algolia({"query": "", "hitsPerPage": 0, "facets": ["categories"],
                 "maxValuesPerFacet": 100})
    return r["facets"]["categories"], r["nbHits"]


def rank_in(cat, store_position):
    """Browse rank of an Actor with this storePosition inside `cat`, and the category size."""
    total = algolia({"query": "", "hitsPerPage": 0,
                     "facetFilters": [[f"categories:{cat}"]]})["nbHits"]
    better = algolia({"query": "", "hitsPerPage": 0,
                      "facetFilters": [[f"categories:{cat}"]],
                      "numericFilters": [f"storePosition<{store_position}"]})["nbHits"]
    return better + 1, total


def our_records():
    """Indexed Algolia record per fetchsmith Actor (has storePosition + live categories)."""
    r = algolia({"query": "fetchsmith", "hitsPerPage": 1000})
    return {h["name"]: h for h in r["hits"] if h.get("username") == "fetchsmith"}


def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    show_all = "--all" in args
    only = [a for a in args if not a.startswith("-")]

    sizes, store_total = facet_sizes()

    if "--facets" in args:
        print(f"store-wide: {store_total} listings\n")
        for cat, n in sorted(sizes.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1]):
            print(f"{n:7d}  {cat}")
        return

    recs = our_records()
    slugs = only or [a["slug"] for a in json.load(open(REG))["tools"]]

    if "--ceiling" in args:
        # Page-1 feasibility, MEASURED -- not modelled.
        #
        # Do NOT try to shortcut this with a store-wide storePosition percentile: those
        # counts come back exhaustiveNbHits=False and are badly wrong, not just noisy.
        # Measured 1620: `storePosition<34312` reported 80472 of 86132 listings, which is
        # arithmetically impossible (only 69255 listings are outside ECOMMERCE, and just
        # 7092 ECOMMERCE members rank ahead of that same Actor). Small categories DO come
        # back exhaustive, so per-category ranks are sound -- only the store-wide
        # denominator is unusable. Hence: probe the K smallest real categories directly.
        PAGE = 24
        probe = [c for _, c in sorted((n, c) for c, n in sizes.items())[:K_PROBE]]
        micro = [c for c in probe if sizes[c] <= PAGE + 5]
        print(f"store-wide: {store_total} listings;  browse page size assumed {PAGE}")
        print(f"probing the {len(probe)} smallest categories: "
              + ", ".join(f"{c}({sizes[c]})" for c in probe))
        print("page-1-feasible categories store-wide (small enough for ANY storePosition): "
              + ", ".join(f"{c}({sizes[c]})" for c in micro) + "\n")
        print(f"{'actor':<32}{'storePos':>9}  {'best rank among its CURRENT cats':<34}page 1 now?")
        for slug in slugs:
            h = recs.get(slug)
            if not h:
                continue
            sp = h.get("storePosition", 999999)
            mine = h.get("categories") or []
            scored = []
            for cat in mine:
                r, t = rank_in(cat, sp)
                scored.append((r, t, cat))
            if not scored:
                print(f"{slug:<32}{sp:>9}  (no categories set)".ljust(79) + "no")
                continue
            r, t, cat = min(scored)
            print(f"{slug:<32}{sp:>9}  {cat}: p{r} of {t}".ljust(79)
                  + ("YES" if r <= PAGE else "no"))
        print(f"""
Any Actor reading "no" above can only reach page 1 by joining one of the micro-
categories listed at the top -- every other category is orders of magnitude too large
for our storePosition. That is a TOPICAL-FIT question, not a rank question, and this
tool deliberately does not recommend one: COVID_19 is honest for the 5 health/gov
Actors that already hold it and would be spam on e.g. steam-reviews, and
DEVELOPER_EXAMPLES is dishonest fleet-wide (we sell products, not demos) -- CLAUDE.md
rule 1. With no honest micro-category left unclaimed, category_lever is CLOSED:
structurally exhausted, not "not yet due". See the CEILING docstring above.""")
        return

    for slug in slugs:
        h = recs.get(slug)
        if not h:
            print(f"{slug}: NOT IN SEARCH INDEX")
            continue
        sp = h.get("storePosition", 999999)
        mine = h.get("categories") or []
        print(f"\n{slug}  storePosition={sp}")
        for cat in mine:
            r, t = rank_in(cat, sp)
            pct = 100.0 * r / t if t else 0
            print(f"    {cat:<20} p{r} of {t}   (top {pct:.0f}%)")
        if show_all:
            print("    -- what-if (categories we are NOT in, best first) --")
            rows = []
            for cat in sizes:
                if cat in mine:
                    continue
                r, t = rank_in(cat, sp)
                rows.append((r, t, cat))
            for r, t, cat in sorted(rows)[:8]:
                # Joining adds us to the category, so the member count grows by one.
                note = "  <- DISHONEST for us (we sell products, not demos)" \
                    if cat in DISHONEST else ""
                print(f"    {cat:<20} p{r} of {t + 1}{note}")
            print("    NOTE: a good rank here is NOT a reason to file under a category the")
            print("    Actor does not genuinely belong to (CLAUDE.md rule 1); and per the")
            print("    CEILING docstring, only the two 7-listing categories reach page 1.")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
